From 0becc90a2eb8906a9f6e4587c5adc0c4c14588a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Nintu (agent)" <nintu@simple-host.local>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 18:27:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] skill: drive every provider with a bearer token and curl, not
 a CLI

A CLI install on a non-technical organiser's laptop is a version, a login and a
new way to fail. Verified today with a live token that UpCloud needs none:
account, plans, zones and the Ubuntu 24.04 template all answer plain curl. All
five HTTP providers answer 401 unauthenticated, so their endpoints are real and
token-driven. Oracle stays on its CLI because it signs every request with an RSA
key pair.

The reference now covers Hetzner, DigitalOcean, UpCloud, Vultr, Hostinger and
Oracle, each with where to click for a token, the create and read-back calls,
the smallest sensible instance and its price, and the one thing that goes wrong
there.

Teardown for every provider is the point of the rewrite. It previously covered
UpCloud alone, so an agent that created a server anywhere else had no documented
way to delete it. Two traps are now stated everywhere: the disk usually survives
deleting the server, and a reserved address can outlive it too. Hostinger has no
published delete call at all, only disabling renewal, so an organiser must not
be told their bill stops today.

Everything except UpCloud is marked untested, because UpCloud is the only one
actually run end to end.

